we end this difficult week for our  country with a powerful reflection on  loss and how our nation remembers as we  head into memorial day weekend cbs  sunday morning correspondent lee cowan  shares an essay on what he calls our  national  wound when there's a deep wound in this  country  we try to heal it by giving it a day  this weekend is memorial day for example  the day to remember all the service men  and women we've lost on active duty  we celebrate their lives all of those  who aren't with us anymore  to never forget  is woven into our dna  [Music]  we do the same every september when we  pause as a bell tolls for every person  murdered on 9 11.  even those who died of covet have been  remembered on the national mall  and yet  for those we've lost to mass shootings  the ones shot in schools  in workplaces  in churches and in nightclubs  we have no national day of remembrance  there's no monument no grand fountain  no place for us to mourn as a nation  why  well  maybe our gun violence is a scar too  painful to face  it's too random too unthinkable it's  certainly too fresh this is tough  aaron was a good man  a good person  and we all deserve better families  hadn't even buried those lost in the  buffalo shooting before the shooting in  uvalde started  mass shootings have become so common  that  perhaps they're not considered  extraordinary enough to single out  anymore  instead makeshift memorials will have to  do they always spring up in the days  that follow our tragedies  they grow  but then they inevitably dwindle the  candles burn out leaving only a wisp of  smoke  but mass shootings are a national wound  and wounds this deep don't just heal on  their own especially if all we do is  pick at it she dropped her up at school  i didn't know it was gonna be the last  time i would see her we blame  politicians who have adjourned for the  holiday by the way taking with them any  urgency to this matter  we also villainize guns we bemoan the  holes in our mental health system  and around and around it goes  it's a given there will be more gun  violence the only question is where  and how close to home evil will visit  next time  we become a nation constantly looking  over our shoulders when perhaps what we  need to be doing  is looking each other in the eye  finding the courage to bring all of this  to an end  maybe if that day ever comes we can  celebrate that victory  with parades and barbecues too  lee cowan's so well said i look forward  to that i think we should remind people  that all these shootings are preventable  that's why this is so hard as he said  the courage to present this unthinkable  and still so fresh
 

